Port notes: fido2-manage 0.2.0
================================

Source and build
----------------

This port uses the Rust CLI in token2-piv-gui/src-tauri/src/bin/fido2-manage.rs
from upstream tag 0.2.0. It does not use the retired Python/C++ implementation
on the legacy branch. The CLI and GUI shared one Cargo package upstream. The
patches add Cargo features so OpenBSD can build the CLI without enabling the GUI,
PIV and OTP modules. Upstream GUI source remains in the distfile and is not
removed or rewritten.

Cargo uses the upstream Cargo.lock and the OpenBSD devel/cargo module. The
generated crates.inc and distinfo files are intentionally not included yet;
generate them from the patched source tree with modcargo-gen-crates and makesum
before fetching/building. This keeps the crate set derived from Cargo.lock and
the checksums generated by the ports framework. The lockfile includes optional
GUI packages too; the port selects only the cli feature at build time.
The active CLI crates are serde, serde_json, hex and libc (MIT OR Apache-2.0);
serde derive support is also enabled. tauri-build remains a build-time dependency
in the upstream package. Tauri, hidapi, rfd, PC/SC and the PIV/OTP crypto crates
are optional GUI dependencies and are not activated by this port. The main
application is BSD-2-Clause. Run the cargo-module license target after generating
crates.inc to populate its per-crate license comments before package review.

Native libraries and FIDO transports
------------------------------------

The CLI calls libfido2 through upstream FFI. OpenBSD 8.0 includes libfido2
1.17.0, libcbor, LibreSSL and zlib in the base system; the binary links only
libfido2 as a native library. The port does not build or replace base libraries.

OpenBSD's fido(4) driver exposes USB HID authenticators through /dev/fido/*. The
system libfido2 uses that native interface. The port does not use hidapi,
Linux hidraw/udev, USB-specific Linux APIs, PC/SC, or a daemon. The CLI's
standard CTAP2 operations are available over FIDO HID: authenticator info,
PIN, resident credential management, authenticator configuration, reset, and
bioEnrollment. Each operation depends on the authenticator advertising and
implementing the corresponding CTAP capability.

FIDO over CCID/PC-SC is a separate transport. Upstream's Linux workflow builds a
private libfido2 with USE_PCSC and libfido2-token2.patch. That patch includes a
Token2 OTP-app tunnel and a Token2-only raw APDU API. OpenBSD base libfido2 does
not contain that patch and the security/fido2-tools port currently disables
PC/SC. This port therefore does not claim CCID FIDO support for Token2 keys.
The serial-number lookup using Token2's private APDU API is excluded from the
CLI feature; it is not needed for FIDO management.

GUI, PIV, PKCS#11 and OTP
-------------------------

The upstream GUI is Tauri 2 with GTK/WebKitGTK and a static HTML/CSS/JS
frontend. Upstream documents Windows, macOS and Linux builds, not OpenBSD. Tauri
and Wry have Unix/GTK code, but a working OpenBSD GUI dependency set and runtime
have not been verified. Packaging it as an optional subpackage would require a
separate native dependency and build review, so this port prioritizes the CLI
and preserves the GUI source without building it.

The GUI's PIV layer calls the bundled token2-piv-tool/libt2piv implementation
through FFI and uses PC/SC. Its PKCS#11 provider is libt2cs11. The OTP code
contains Token2-specific HID operations and PC/SC OATH support. These features
are not exposed by fido2-manage and are not built or packaged here. They would
need separately reviewed ports and PC/SC integration; no pcscd service or
permissions are configured automatically. Compatibility of libt2piv's OpenSSL
calls with OpenBSD LibreSSL has not been established.

Authenticator coverage and limitations
---------------------------------------

PIN, resident passkey management, authenticator configuration and biometrics
use libfido2's standard APIs and CTAP commands. This should work with any
manufacturer whose authenticator supports those standard features, subject to
its advertised capabilities. Token2's CCID tunnel, vendor APDU calls, PIV and
OTP are not standard FIDO operations and are not included. THETIS FIDO 2.1
compatibility is unverified on hardware; no device behavior is claimed without
real testing.

Security review
---------------

The CLI defaults to masked interactive PIN entry. PINs supplied with -pin or
-newpin remain visible to process inspection and shell history, as in upstream;
use the prompt instead. PINs are held in ordinary Rust String/CString values
and are not explicitly zeroized. The OpenBSD patch replaces the upstream
handwritten Linux-shaped termios structure/constants with libc's platform-native
termios API and an RAII restoration guard, so terminal flags are restored on
read errors and cancellation. The resident credential delete command requires
an explicit YES confirmation. Factory reset is destructive, has the authenticator
power-up time window, and requires user touch; verify the selected device before
using it. Biometric deletion follows upstream behavior and does not add a
separate confirmation prompt.

Patch summary
-------------

* Cargo manifest/lock: add cli/gui feature selection and libc for native termios;
  keep GUI dependencies optional and select only the CLI for this port.
* build.rs/lib.rs/fido.rs: skip GUI/PIV links and modules in CLI builds, retain
  the shared FIDO session implementation, and avoid Token2-only APDU symbols.
* fido2-manage.rs: use OpenBSD-compatible termios layout/flags and restore the
  terminal on all normal error paths.

